  "type": "object",
  "required": ["customerId"],
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "properties": {"customerId": {"type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "x-in": "path", "x-expression": "customerId"}}
}</camunda:inputParameter>
          <camunda:inputParameter name="headers" />
          <camunda:inputParameter name="jwt">accessToken</camunda:inputParameter>
          <camunda:inputParameter name="responseSchema" />
          <camunda:inputParameter name="resultVariable">shipping</camunda:inputParameter>
          <camunda:inputParameter name="statusVariable" />
        </camunda:inputOutput>
      </bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>in_shipping</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>out_shipping</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:serviceTask>
    <bpmn:serviceTask id="getConsents" name="GET consents" camunda:delegateExpression="${restGet}">
      <bpmn:documentation>[REST connector] REST GET - Fetch data: request params built from a JSON Schema; 4xx -&gt; BPMN error HTTP_&lt;status&gt;.
Implementation: delegateExpression ${restGet}
Inputs (leave empty when not needed):
- url: Request URL. Supports ${expressions} and {placeholders} that are filled from params with "x-in": "path". Example: ${apiBaseUrl}/users/{userId}
- sources: Comma separated process variable names (or a JSON literal) deep-merged in order - later wins. Example: currentUser, userChanges
- paramsSchema: JSON Schema for path / query / header params. Each top-level property is one param: "x-in": path | query | header (default query); arrays repeat, objects flatten to name.child. Paste the JSON Schema itself (inline JSON object), not a file path. Use "x-expression": "varName" (no dollar-brace wrapper) for expressions - the engine evaluates any dollar-brace text in this field before the connector runs. Keep it under ~4000 characters (engine string-variable limit).
- headers: JSON object, values may use ${expressions}. Example: {"X-Correlation-Id": "${execution.processInstanceId}"}
- jwt: Optional. Sends Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;. Give the NAME of the process variable that holds the token (e.g. accessToken) - preferred, the token is read at runtime and no copy is stored in history. A token literal or the result of an expression also works. Overrides an Authorization header from Static headers / params. HTTP 401/403 -&gt; BPMN error HTTP_401 / HTTP_403.
- responseSchema: Optional JSON Schema: shapes the response (drops unknown fields) and validates it. Violation -&gt; BPMN error RESPONSE_INVALID. Paste the JSON Schema itself (inline JSON object), not a file path. Use "x-expression": "varName" (no dollar-brace wrapper) for expressions - the engine evaluates any dollar-brace text in this field before the connector runs. Keep it under ~4000 characters (engine string-variable limit).
- resultVariable: Process variable receiving the response body (stored as JSON).
